---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import CnmlSeal from '../components/CnmlSeal.astro';
---

<Base title="CNML">
  <!-- Section 1: What CNML is -->
  <section class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6 pt-20 pb-16">
    <div class="cnml-seal-wrap mb-12">
      <CnmlSeal />
    </div>
    <h1 class="cnml-hero__title mb-6">
      Certificat Numérique de Métrologie Légale
    </h1>
    <p class="cnml-hero__lede">
      CNML is a cryptographically-signed digital certificate format for OIML type approvals, developed under the OIML SMART program. It provides machine-verifiable certification of measuring instruments subject to legal metrology, built on recognized international standards including X.509 v3, W3C XMLDSig, and NIST FIPS 204.
    </p>
    <p class="text-[var(--ink-soft)] text-lg leading-relaxed mt-6">
      CNML covers all 22 OIML Recommendations governing measuring instruments used in trade, health, safety, and environmental protection. Each certificate binds a type-approval evaluation to a cryptographic signature produced by a distributed threshold quorum of international signers. Any party can verify a CNML certificate in a web browser without contacting the issuer.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- Section 2: Why CNML exists -->
  <section class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6 py-16 border-t border-[var(--rule)]">
    <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl font-semibold text-[var(--ink)] mb-6">Why CNML exists</h2>
    <div class="space-y-4 text-[var(--ink-soft)] leading-relaxed">
      <p>
        Legal metrology governs measuring instruments used in trade, health, safety, and environmental protection. Fuel dispensers, electricity meters, weighbridges, medical thermometers, exhaust gas analyzers, and pressure gauges all require type approval before they can be lawfully placed on the market. Type approval is an international process: an OIML-Designated Issuing Authority evaluates a model against an OIML Recommendation and issues a certificate of conformity.
      </p>
      <p>
        That certificate must be verifiable by any party. A market-surveillance authority inspecting an instrument at a remote site needs to confirm the type approval. A manufacturer shipping instruments across borders needs to demonstrate conformity. Another Issuing Authority participating in the OIML Declaration of Mutual Confidence needs to recognize the certificate. The certificate must remain valid for the service lifetime of the instrument, which in legal metrology is measured in decades.
      </p>
      <p>
        No existing digital certificate technology meets all of these requirements simultaneously. Web TLS public-key infrastructure is designed for short-lived, high-volume channel authentication, not for decades-long document signing under international governance. Single-key PKI concentrates trust in one operator and cannot distribute signing authority across sovereign institutions. CNML was designed from the ground up for the specific requirements of legal-metrology type approval.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Section 3: What CNML enables -->
  <section class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6 py-16 border-t border-[var(--rule)]">
    <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl font-semibold text-[var(--ink)] mb-8">What CNML enables</h2>
    <div class="space-y-10">
      <div>
        <h3 class="font-sans text-lg font-semibold text-[var(--ink)] mb-3">Distributed signing authority</h3>
        <p class="text-[var(--ink-soft)] leading-relaxed">
          No single party can produce a CNML certificate at the root or Issuing Authority tier. Signing authority is distributed across an international threshold quorum of directors and officers using FROST threshold signatures. A court order directed at one officer cannot complete a signature. Theft of one key holder's hardware cannot forge a certificate. The aggregate public key survives director rotation, so all previously issued certificates remain valid without re-issuance.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3 class="font-sans text-lg font-semibold text-[var(--ink)] mb-3">Verification by anyone, anywhere</h3>
        <p class="text-[var(--ink-soft)] leading-relaxed">
          Any party can verify a CNML certificate in a web browser, offline, without contacting the issuer, registering for a service, or holding an account. The verifier downloads a trust-anchor bundle once and can then verify certificates indefinitely. The verification pipeline checks XML structure, schema conformance, signature validity, issuer scope, revocation status, timestamp proof, and transparency-log inclusion.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3 class="font-sans text-lg font-semibold text-[var(--ink)] mb-3">Post-quantum security</h3>
        <p class="text-[var(--ink-soft)] leading-relaxed">
          CNML ships composite signatures combining Ed25519 (classical, RFC 8032) with ML-DSA-65 (post-quantum, NIST FIPS 204). A composite signature is valid only when both components verify. An attacker must break both algorithms to forge a signature. Documents signed today remain secure through the transition to quantum computing.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3 class="font-sans text-lg font-semibold text-[var(--ink)] mb-3">Public transparency</h3>
        <p class="text-[var(--ink-soft)] leading-relaxed">
          Every issued certificate appears in a public Merkle transparency log. Tree roots are anchored to Bitcoin through OpenTimestamps. No certificate can be silently issued or retroactively inserted. The log is mirrored across independent operators, and gossip protocols prevent equivocation. A verifier that demands an inclusion proof rejects any certificate that did not appear in the log.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Section 4: How the technology works -->
  <section class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6 py-16 border-t border-[var(--rule)]">
    <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl font-semibold text-[var(--ink)] mb-6">How the technology works</h2>
    <div class="space-y-4 text-[var(--ink-soft)] leading-relaxed">
      <p>
        CNML organizes signing authority in a five-tier certificate hierarchy. The BIML Root tier holds the international root signing key as a threshold secret shared among OIML directors. The Issuing Authority tier holds per-national-authority intermediate keys as threshold secrets shared among IA officers. The Test Laboratory tier signs measurement reports. The Manufacturer Model tier delegates scoped signing authority to instrument manufacturers. The Manufacturer Instance tier issues per-instrument end-entity certificates.
      </p>
      <p>
        At the BIML Root and IA tiers, every signature requires a threshold quorum. Directors and officers participate asynchronously through a coordinator service that buffers protocol messages, so participants in different time zones can contribute when convenient. The coordinator is honest-but-curious: it observes encrypted protocol messages but cannot reconstruct the signing key.
      </p>
      <p>
        BIML binds each Issuing Authority to a specific subset of OIML Recommendations through an X.509 v3 extension. The extension is enforced at verification time, so an IA scoped to load cells cannot produce a valid fuel-dispenser certificate. The scope is a cryptographic property, not a policy agreement.
      </p>
      <p>
        The full architecture, including the transparency log, the hardware-backed key tiers, and the post-quantum migration path, is developed in the <a href="/docs" class="text-[var(--accent)] underline">documentation</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Section 5: Who uses CNML and how -->
  <section class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6 py-16 border-t border-[var(--rule)]">
    <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl font-semibold text-[var(--ink)] mb-8">Who uses CNML</h2>
    <div class="space-y-6 text-[var(--ink-soft)] leading-relaxed">
      <div>
        <span class="font-sans font-semibold text-[var(--ink)]">Issuing Authorities</span>
        <span class="text-[var(--ink-muted)]"> issue type-approval certificates within their authorized scope.</span>
      </div>
      <div>
        <span class="font-sans font-semibold text-[var(--ink)]">BIML directors</span>
        <span class="text-[var(--ink-muted)]"> participate in the threshold quorum that signs IA certificates.</span>
      </div>
      <div>
        <span class="font-sans font-semibold text-[var(--ink)]">Test laboratories</span>
        <span class="text-[var(--ink-muted)]"> sign measurement reports and submit them as type-evaluation evidence.</span>
      </div>
      <div>
        <span class="font-sans font-semibold text-[var(--ink)]">Manufacturers</span>
        <span class="text-[var(--ink-muted)]"> issue per-instance certificates under a scoped model delegation.</span>
      </div>
      <div>
        <span class="font-sans font-semibold text-[var(--ink)]">Market-surveillance authorities</span>
        <span class="text-[var(--ink-muted)]"> verify certificates in the field using a browser, offline.</span>
      </div>
      <div>
        <span class="font-sans font-semibold text-[var(--ink)]">OIML member states</span>
        <span class="text-[var(--ink-muted)]"> evaluate CNML for national adoption under the DoMC framework.</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Section 6: App area -->
  <section class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6 py-16 border-t border-[var(--rule)]">
    <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl font-semibold text-[var(--ink)] mb-4">Try it</h2>
    <p class="text-[var(--ink-soft)] leading-relaxed mb-6">
      The interactive tools generate sample CNML certificates for evaluation purposes. They run entirely in your browser. Private keys are generated on device, encrypted with a passphrase-derived key, and never leave your machine.
    </p>
    <a href="/app" class="cnml-btn cnml-btn-primary inline-flex">
      Open the certificate operations app
    </a>
  </section>
</Base>
